---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import BlurFaceInPhoto from '@/components/tools/image-edit/BlurFaceInPhoto.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="censor-image">
  <BlurFaceInPhoto client:load defaultEffect="block" defaultShape="rect" subject="anything" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Censoring a screenshot properly</h2>
    <p>
      Most leaks of “redacted” material come from one mistake: the black rectangle was drawn as an
      object on top of the content and did not replace it. In PDFs, slide decks, design files and many
      screenshot annotators, that rectangle is a separate layer. Select it, delete it, and the original
      is below it, intact. This page overwrites the pixels inside your box before the file is encoded,
      so there is nothing to remove.
    </p>

    <h2>How to use it</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop in the screenshot, photo or scan.</li>
      <li>Drag a rectangle over each item that must not be published: names, emails, account numbers, addresses, API keys, faces.</li>
      <li>Keep the effect on <strong>Solid block</strong> for text. Use blur or mosaic only for faces and scenery.</li>
      <li>Check the preview, then export. The result is re-encoded from raw pixels, so the metadata is removed.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>A checklist for screenshots you are about to post</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The obvious content: the figure, the name, the message you ask about.</li>
      <li>The browser chrome. Tabs show the other pages you had open. The address bar can contain session tokens and internal hostnames. A bookmarks bar shows much about your employer.</li>
      <li>Notifications and the clock: a banner from a private group, an email subject line, a timestamp that shows where you were.</li>
      <li>Autocomplete and history: an open dropdown below a search field is a common source of accidental disclosure.</li>
      <li>Reflections and second copies: a monitor in a phone photo, a preview pane, a thumbnail strip.</li>
      <li>Usernames in file paths: <code>C:\Users\firstname.lastname\…</code> in a terminal or a title bar identifies you.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why solid black beats blur for anything textual</h2>
    <p>
      Blur and mosaic are deterministic filters. When the content is short structured text, the number
      of possible values is small. If someone thinks the hidden value is a six-digit order number,
      they can render all million candidates, apply the same filter and compare. This has been done
      many times on real redacted documents. In a filled rectangle all pixels have the same value, so
      no computation can recover the content. Use blur only for faces, where the candidates cannot be
      listed.
    </p>

    <h2>Colour choice</h2>
    <p>
      Black is the convention and is clear. White can look tidier on a light screenshot, but it also
      looks like an empty field, so the reader can think nothing was there. For a report or a bug
      ticket, black with a visible border shows that the redaction is deliberate. To show different
      categories of redaction, use two clearly different colours and explain them in the caption.
    </p>

    <h2>What happens to the file itself</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Concern</th><th>What this tool does</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Hidden layers</td><td>None exist. The output is a flat raster encoded from the canvas.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Undo history</td><td>Not written to the file. Some editors embed edit history or a preview of the previous state.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>EXIF / GPS</td><td>Discarded, because the file is built from pixels and not patched.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Embedded thumbnail</td><td>Discarded. This item most often leaks the image from before the redaction.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Upload</td><td>Never happens. The image is opened, drawn and encoded in this tab.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Working with documents rather than images</h2>
    <p>
      If your source is a PDF, a screenshot of it is a workaround. It is more thorough to redact the
      PDF itself: rasterise the affected pages and remove the text layer. For this reason, the
      <a href="/redact-pdf">PDF redaction tool</a> destroys the text layer and does not only cover it.
      Use this page for images and that tool for contracts and discovery material.
    </p>

    <h2>Related tools</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/blur-face-in-photo">Blur Face in Photo</a> for people,
      <a href="/pixelate-image">Pixelate Image</a> for a mosaic look, and
      <a href="/remove-exif-data">Remove EXIF Data</a> when the pixels are fine but the metadata is
      not.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
